    detailedAnalysisEn: "To understand Islamic inheritance without confusion, one must realize that God\ndivided human relationships into two distinct realms:\n\n                                  ### The Dual Grounds of Inheritance (موجبات و اسبابِ ارث)\n\nAL-SABAB (السَّبَب) — Contractual Affinity | AL-NASAB (النَّسَب) — Biological Lineage\n:--- | :---\n**Primary Heirs:** Husband (الزوج) & Wife (الزوجة) | **Primary Heirs:** Parents, Children, Siblings, Uncles\nOperates strictly via permanent marriage (*Nikāḥ Dā’im*) | Structured into 3 Mutually Exclusive Tiers\n**Immunity:** Never blocked by any human relative on Earth | **Exclusion:** Governed by Total Exclusion (*Ḥujub al-Ḥirmān*)\nAlways carves out their fixed Quranic share first | Divides remaining estate after spousal share is deducted\n\nGround 1: Al-Sabab (السبب — Matrimonial Contract)\n\n  - Definition: A legal bond created by human agreement through a valid,\n    permanent marriage contract (Nikāḥ Dā’im).\n  - The Golden Rules of Spousal Title:\n    1.  Universal Co-Existence: Spouses inherit alongside Tier 1\n        (Parents/Children), alongside Tier 2 (Grandparents/Siblings), and\n        alongside Tier 3 (Uncles/Cousins).\n    2.  Immunity to Exclusion: No relative on earth—whether a mother, father,\n        son, or brother—can ever strip a husband or wife of their statutory\n        right.\n    3.  The Temporary Marriage Nuance (Nikāḥ al-Munqaṭi‘ / Mut‘ah): In\n        mainstream Ja‘farī jurisprudence, a wife in a fixed-term marriage\n        (Mut‘ah) does not inherit statutory inheritance, nor does the husband\n        inherit from her, even if stipulated, based on the reliable verdicts of\n        Ayatollah Sistani and Ayatollah Khoei.\n    4.  The Divorced Spouse (Ṭalāq):\n          - If the divorce was revocable (Raj‘ī), and either spouse dies during\n            the waiting period (‘Iddah), they inherit from each other fully, as\n            the marriage bond is legally active.\n          - If the divorce was irrevocable (Bā’in), mutual inheritance is\n            immediately severed.\n          - The Terminal Illness Exception (Maraḍ al-Mawt): If a husband\n            divorces his wife while suffering from a terminal disease from which\n            he later dies, the divorced wife inherits from him for up to one\n            full lunar year, provided she did not remarry and he did not recover\n            from that illness.\n\nGround 2: Al-Nasab (النسب — Consanguineous Bloodline)\n\n  - Definition: Biological relationship established through legitimate birth.\n  - The Nature of Bloodline: Blood relatives cannot all inherit simultaneously.\n    Because human lineages expand exponentially, allowing every cousin, nephew,\n    and uncle to inherit alongside children would shatter estates into\n    meaningless fragments. Therefore, the Divine Law structured blood relatives\n    into Three Non-Negotiable Tiers.",
    detailedAnalysisUr: "To understand Islamic inheritance without confusion, one must realize that God\ndivided human relationships into two distinct realms:\n\n                                  ### The Dual Grounds of Inheritance (موجبات و اسبابِ ارث)\n\nAL-SABAB (السَّبَب) — Contractual Affinity | AL-NASAB (النَّسَب) — Biological Lineage\n:--- | :---\n**Primary Heirs:** Husband (الزوج) & Wife (الزوجة) | **Primary Heirs:** Parents, Children, Siblings, Uncles\nOperates strictly via permanent marriage (*Nikāḥ Dā’im*) | Structured into 3 Mutually Exclusive Tiers\n**Immunity:** Never blocked by any human relative on Earth | **Exclusion:** Governed by Total Exclusion (*Ḥujub al-Ḥirmān*)\nAlways carves out their fixed Quranic share first | Divides remaining estate after spousal share is deducted\n\nGround 1: Al-Sabab (السبب — Matrimonial Contract)\n\n  - Definition: A legal bond created by human agreement through a valid,\n    permanent marriage contract (Nikāḥ Dā’im).\n  - The Golden Rules of Spousal Title:\n    1.  Universal Co-Existence: Spouses inherit alongside Tier 1\n        (Parents/Children), alongside Tier 2 (Grandparents/Siblings), and\n        alongside Tier 3 (Uncles/Cousins).\n    2.  Immunity to Exclusion: No relative on earth—whether a mother, father,\n        son, or brother—can ever strip a husband or wife of their statutory\n        right.\n    3.  The Temporary Marriage Nuance (Nikāḥ al-Munqaṭi‘ / Mut‘ah): In\n        mainstream Ja‘farī jurisprudence, a wife in a fixed-term marriage\n        (Mut‘ah) does not inherit statutory inheritance, nor does the husband\n        inherit from her, even if stipulated, based on the reliable verdicts of\n        Ayatollah Sistani and Ayatollah Khoei.\n    4.  The Divorced Spouse (Ṭalāq):\n          - If the divorce was revocable (Raj‘ī), and either spouse dies during\n            the waiting period (‘Iddah), they inherit from each other fully, as\n            the marriage bond is legally active.\n          - If the divorce was irrevocable (Bā’in), mutual inheritance is\n            immediately severed.\n          - The Terminal Illness Exception (Maraḍ al-Mawt): If a husband\n            divorces his wife while suffering from a terminal disease from which\n            he later dies, the divorced wife inherits from him for up to one\n            full lunar year, provided she did not remarry and he did not recover\n            from that illness.\n\nGround 2: Al-Nasab (النسب — Consanguineous Bloodline)\n\n  - Definition: Biological relationship established through legitimate birth.\n  - The Nature of Bloodline: Blood relatives cannot all inherit simultaneously.\n    Because human lineages expand exponentially, allowing every cousin, nephew,\n    and uncle to inherit alongside children would shatter estates into\n    meaningless fragments. Therefore, the Divine Law structured blood relatives\n    into Three Non-Negotiable Tiers.",
