    executiveSummaryEn: "Islamic succession does not treat inheritance in isolation; it functions as one component of an integrated economic macro-system. The apparent arithmetic disparity of the 2:1 sibling ratio reflects gross allocation versus net legal liability. Islam awards the male heir a larger gross portion because he is placed under strict, lifetime financial obligations: mandatory nuptial gifts (Mahr), full living maintenance (Nafaqah) of wife and offspring, and parental care. In contrast, the female heir receives a smaller gross portion as 100% unencumbered, private capital—she is legally exempt from spending a single penny on herself, her children, or her household. Furthermore, multiple wives share their fixed Quranic portion (1/8 or 1/4) equally (Bi al-Sawiyyah), with real estate rights strictly defined by contemporary Marja‘ fatwas.",
    executiveSummaryUr: "اسلام کا نظامِ وراثت تنہا کام نہیں کرتا بلکہ یہ شریعت کے ہمہ گیر معاشی اور مالیاتی نظام کا ایک بنیادی ستون ہے۔ بھائی اور بہن کے حصوں میں ۲ اور ۱ کا ظاہری فرق درحقیقت مجموعی آمدنی اور لازمی اخراجات کا توازن ہے۔ شریعت نے مرد کو زیادہ حصہ اس لیے دیا کیونکہ اس کی گردن پر تاحیات قانونی واجبات کا بوجھ ہے: بیوی کا مہر، بیوی اور بچوں کے تمام تر اخراجات و نفقہ (رہائش، کھانا، علاج)، اور بوڑھے والدین کی کفالت۔ اس کے برعکس، عورت کو ملنے والا ترکہ ۱۰۰ فیصد خالص، ذاتی اور محفوظ بچت ہے؛ اس پر اپنے نان نفقہ، بچوں کی روٹی، یا گھر کے بلوں کی ادائیگی کی صفر فیصد شرعی ذمہ داری ہے۔ مزید برآں، ایک سے زائد بیویاں اپنے قرآنی حصے (۱/۸ یا ۱/۴) میں بالکل برابر کی شریک ہوتی ہیں، اور زمین کے حوالے سے مراجعِ عظام کے فتاویٰ کے مطابق حق پاتی ہیں۔",
    detailedAnalysisEn: "A. The Economic Fallacy of the Secular Critique\n\nSecular critics compare the gross receipts of brother (10) and sister (5) and\nconclude that Islam favors men. This is an accounting fallacy that audits gross\nrevenues while ignoring non-negotiable statutory liabilities:\n\n\nTHE COMPREHENSIVE CASH-FLOW LEDGER\n├──────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────┤\nTHE MALE SUCCESSOR                       │ THE FEMALE SUCCESSOR\n├──────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n• Gross Inheritance: 2 Shares (5,000)\n• Nuptial Debt (Mahr to bride): -3,000\n• Lifetime Nafaqah (Wife/Children):      │ • Personal Subsistence: $0\n-$6,000                                │   (100% funded by husband or father)\n• Indigent Parent Maintenance: -$1,000   │ • Household/Domestic Outflows: $0\n• Blood Money Liability (Diyah): Oblig.  │ • Enterprise/Salary: 100% Private Wealth\n├──────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────┤\nNET RETAINED CAPITAL: $0 to $500         │ NET RETAINED CAPITAL: $8,000+\n(Living under constant financial stress) │ (Total legal immunity from expenses)\n└──────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\nB. The Sacred Inflow Streams of a Muslim Woman\n\nA woman in Islamic law is a financial sovereign. She enjoys four distinct\nfinancial streams with zero mandatory domestic expenditures:\n\n1.  Al-Mahr (المهر): Mandatory financial endowment transferred directly into her\n    sole ownership upon marriage.\n2.  Al-Nafaqah (النفقة): Complete, legally actionable maintenance encompassing\n    food, bespoke clothing, independent housing, healthcare, and domestic\n    domestic assistance where customary. Even if a woman is an\n    ultra-high-net-worth billionaire and her husband is working-class, it\n    remains unlawful for him to demand that she buy her own medicine or bread.\n3.  Al-Irth (الإرث): Multiple statutory succession rights as a daughter, mother,\n    wife, sister, and grandmother.\n4.  Al-Milkiyyah al-Khāṣṣah (الملكية الخاصة): Absolute private property rights\n    since 610 CE. She can earn, invest, trade, or gift her assets without\n    seeking the permission of father, brother, or husband.\n\nC. The Jurisprudence of Multiple Wives (تعددِ ازواج)\n\nWhen a deceased husband leaves between two and four valid permanent wives:\n\n1.  The Principle of Equal Partition (Bi al-Sawiyyah — بالسوية): The wives do\n    not receive separate fractions. The collective spousal portion (1/8th if the\n    deceased has children from any marriage; 1/4th if childless) is divided\n    equally among the surviving permanent wives:\n      - 2 Wives with children: Each receives 1/16th (1/8 ÷ 2).\n      - 3 Wives with children: Each receives 1/24th (1/8 ÷ 3).\n      - 4 Wives with children: Each receives 1/32nd (1/8 ÷ 4).\n2.  Irrelevance of Children’s Maternity: A childless second wife receives the\n    exact same fractional share of the 1/8th pool as the first wife who bore ten\n    sons.\n\nD. The Real Estate Divide: Land vs. Buildings (Mas’alat Arāḍī al-Zawjah)\n\nOne of the most consequential fatwa divergences in contemporary Ja‘farī\njurisprudence concerns the widow’s inheritance from real property:\n\n\nCONTEMPORARY MARJA‘ DIVERGENCE: WIDOW'S REAL ESTATE TITLE\n├──────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────┤\nSCHOOL OF SAYYID SISTANI & KHAMENEI      │ SCHOOL OF SAYYID KHOEI & CLASSICAL JURISTS\n├──────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n• Inherits from the MONETARY VALUE of    │ • Inherits ZERO from the land itself\nALL land (Residential, Commercial,     │   (neither physical soil nor its valuation).\nand Agricultural).                     │\n• Also inherits from the monetary value  │ • Inherits strictly from the MONETARY VALUE\nof buildings, fixtures, and trees.     │   of building structures, trees & chattels.\n• Note: She does not take physical title │ • Other heirs appraise the bricks/trees and\nto the soil; heirs must pay her cash.  │   pay her fractional cash value.\n└──────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────┘",
