    executiveSummaryEn: "Ja‘farī jurisprudence accounts for non-standard life events with high mathematical and legal precision. An unborn fetus (Al-Ḥaml) is guaranteed an escrow reserve equal to the portion of two male twins until birth establishes life. In simultaneous fatal catastrophes where the chronological sequence of death cannot be verified (Al-Gharqā wa al-Hadmā), the law executes a bilateral cross-succession, presuming each relative died before the other based solely on their original, unacquired estates. Finally, multi-generational estates left undivided for decades are untangled through Compounded Succession (Al-Munāsakhāt), tracking legal titles through modular algebraic fractions down surviving branches.",
    executiveSummaryUr: "فقہِ جعفریہ نے انسانی زندگی کے غیر معمولی اور پیچیدہ ترین حادثات کے لیے انتہائی باریک اور فولادی قوانین وضع کیے ہیں۔ ماں کے پیٹ میں موجود بچے (حمل) کی وراثت کو محفوظ کرنے کے لیے پیدائش تک کم از کم دو جڑواں لڑکوں کا حصہ امانت کے طور پر روکا جاتا ہے۔ اگر رشتہ دار کسی مشترکہ حادثے (جہاز یا گاڑی کا حادثہ، عمارت کا گرنا) میں اکھٹے جاں بحق ہو جائیں اور یہ معلوم نہ ہو سکے کہ پہلے کون مرا اور بعد میں کون، تو شریعت دوطرفہ متوازی تقسیم کا نفاذ کرتی ہے جس میں ہر ایک کو دوسرے کا وارث اس کے پرانے ذاتی مال میں بنایا جاتا ہے۔ مزید برآں، وہ جائیدادیں جو دہائیوں تک تقسیم نہ ہوں اور ایک کے بعد دوسرا وارث مرتا رہے، انہیں مناسخات کے ریاضیاتی اصول کے تحت پرانی اور نئی وراثت کی نسبتیں جوڑ کر بغیر کسی حق تلفی کے حل کیا جاتا ہے۔",
    detailedAnalysisEn: "A. The Unborn Child (Al-Ḥaml — الجنين و الحمل)\n\n  - The Legal Status: A child in the womb possesses conditional ownership from\n    the moment of conception.\n  - The Two Conditions for Title:\n    1.  Conception must have occurred prior to the death of the testator.\n    2.  The child must be born alive (Ḥayyan). If the infant is delivered\n        stillborn or dies inside the womb, it receives 0, and its reserved\n        portion reverts to the other heirs. If it takes even a single breath,\n        cries, or moves visibly after complete delivery, it captures its full\n        inheritance and—if it subsequently dies minutes later—passes that wealth\n        to its own heirs (such as the mother).\n  - The Escrow Rule (‘Azl al-Ḥiṣṣah): If heirs insist on dividing the estate\n    immediately before the mother gives birth, the administrator must place in\n    escrow the maximum possible claim—standardly calculated as the share of two\n    male heirs (twins). If a single girl is born, her 1 share is transferred to\n    her, and the surplus is redistributed.\n\nB. Simultaneous Fatalities (Al-Gharqā wa al-Hadmā — الغرقى والهدَمى)\n\n  - The Real-World Dilemma: A father and his adult son die in the same car\n    crash, plane crash, or natural catastrophe, and medical examiners cannot\n    determine who drew their last breath first.\n  - The Classical Misconception: If Father died first, Son inherited and passed\n    wealth to his wife. If Son died first, Father inherited and passed wealth to\n    his other brothers.\n  - The Ja‘farī Doctrine of Mutual Cross-Succession (Al-Tawāruth):\n    1.  Jurists decree that each relative is presumed to have died before the\n        other in alternating legal sequences.\n    2.  Father is assumed to have died first: his original estate is divided,\n        awarding the deceased Son his share, which transfers to the Son’s\n        independent heirs.\n    3.  Son is assumed to have died first: his original estate is divided,\n        awarding the deceased Father his share, which transfers to the Father’s\n        independent heirs.\n    4.  The Strict Anti-Looping Rule: Neither relative inherits from the wealth\n        that was just transferred to them from the other during this\n        calculation. Mutual cross-succession applies strictly to their original\n        pre-accident assets (Aṣl al-Māl).\n\nC. Compounded Successions (Al-Munāsakhāt — المناسخات)\n\n  - The Definition: Occurs when an original testator dies, and before the estate\n    is legally partitioned, one or more heirs also pass away, compounding\n    succession across decades.\n  - The Algorithmic Resolution:\n    1.  Step 1: Compute the primary distribution of the original deceased’s\n        estate (Al-Farīḍah al-Ūlā), establishing the fractional share due to the\n        deceased heir.\n    2.  Step 2: Construct a separate, secondary estate ledger (Al-Farīḍah\n        al-Thāniyah) for the deceased heir, identifying their own unique heirs\n        (some of whom may be strangers to the first family, such as a second\n        wife or son-in-law).\n    3.  Step 3: Harmonize the denominators using the Least Common Multiple\n        (Taṣḥīḥ al-Farīḍah), ensuring that the transferred share cascades\n        down the branch without diluting the non-involved primary heirs.",
